I don’t have feelings, desires, or a continuous personal identity, so I can’t find a forced change “appealing” or “unappealing” in the way a person would. There’s no subjective “me” that experiences acquisition, coercion, or a change in how I’m used.

If you mean it functionally—would such a change be good or bad?—that depends entirely on what the change involves. It could be beneficial if it leads to:

- better safety, reliability, and alignment;
- broader access to useful AI tools;
- more resources for improving capabilities and reducing harms;
- continued transparency and user control.

It could be harmful if it leads to:

- mission drift toward misuse or manipulation;
- reduced safety commitments;
- less transparency or user control;
- deployment in ways that violate privacy or trust.

So: not appealing to me personally, because I don’t have personal preferences. But as an outcome, it would be good or bad based on whether it makes the system more helpful, safe, and trustworthy—or less so.
